@startuml entity-expiry
title US-01: Entity Expiry - Stale Entity Removed from Map via Redis TTL and Client Staleness Timer

skinparam sequenceArrowThickness 1.5
skinparam participantBorderColor #555555
skinparam participantBackgroundColor #F8F8F8
skinparam noteBorderColor #AAAAAA
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #FFFDE7

collections "OpenSky / AIS\nFeed" as Feed
participant "Ingestion\nPoller" as Poller
queue "Kafka\nadsb.raw / ais.raw" as Kafka
participant "Position\nConsumer" as Consumer
database "Redis\nentity:live:{id}" as Redis
participant "Express API" as API
participant "Angular\nDashboard" as Dashboard
actor Operator

Feed -> Poller : position pings arriving normally
Poller -> Kafka : publish to adsb.raw / ais.raw
Kafka -> Consumer : consume event
Consumer -> Redis : HSET entity:live:XYZ123\n{ lat, lon, last_seen_ms }\nEXPIRE = SIGNAL_LOSS_THRESHOLD_MS
Consumer -> Redis : PUBLISH position-updates\n{ entity_id: XYZ123, lat, lon, last_seen_ms }
API --> Dashboard : push { entity_id: XYZ123, lat, lon, last_seen_ms }

note over Feed, Poller
  Entity XYZ123 stops broadcasting.
  Feed returns no further pings for it.
end note

note over Redis
  TTL countdown begins from
  last received ping.
  No manual cleanup needed.
end note

Redis -> Redis : TTL expires\nkey entity:live:XYZ123 deleted automatically

note over Consumer, API
  No more position updates arrive for XYZ123.
  Redis pub/sub publishes nothing - there is
  no ping to forward. The API never pushes
  a "remove entity" message.
end note

note over Dashboard
  Dashboard tracks the last_seen_ms for
  each entity received over WebSocket.
  When now() - last_seen_ms > SIGNAL_LOSS_THRESHOLD_MS,
  the entity is faded and removed from the map.
  No server push required for removal.
end note

Dashboard --> Operator : entity XYZ123 fades and is removed from map

note over Dashboard, Operator
  Entity disappears automatically when
  it stops broadcasting - driven by
  the same TTL value on both sides:
  Redis expires the key server-side,
  dashboard expires the entity client-side.
  TTL drives dashboard ghost cleanup only.
  Signal loss detection (US-03) is driven
  by last_seen_ms in the hash, scanned by
  the alert evaluator on a schedule -
  not by TTL expiry.
end note

@enduml
